Only point sys.executable at cross-python while that wrapper exists.

crossenv 1.6.0 added an unconditional

    executable = <venv>/cross/bin/python

to sys-patch.py.tmpl so that subprocesses launched with sys.executable get
cross-python. conda-forge's cross-python copies that wrapper to
<venv>/bin/cross-python, installs a shim at $PREFIX/bin/python and then removes
<venv>/cross, so on every cross build sys.executable named a path that had just
been deleted:

    ERROR: Error [Errno 2] No such file or directory:
      '.../_build_env/venv/cross/bin/python' while executing command
      Preparing metadata (pyproject.toml)

Guarding on the wrapper still being present keeps upstream's behaviour where it
applies and restores the 1.5.0 behaviour otherwise -- sys.executable then stays
the shim that conda-forge put on argv[0], which is itself cross-python.

Not yet reported upstream; the guard is written so it can be sent there as-is.
--- a/crossenv/scripts/sys-patch.py.tmpl
+++ b/crossenv/scripts/sys-patch.py.tmpl
@@ -15,4 +15,12 @@
 
 # If a process started by cross-python tries to start a subprocess with sys.executable,
 # make sure that it points at cross-python.
-executable = {{repr(context.cross_env_exe)}}
+#
+# Only do this while that wrapper is still on disk. An environment is allowed to
+# relocate it and leave its own entry point in place; overwriting sys.executable
+# with a path that no longer exists breaks every subprocess launch instead.
+_cross_env_exe = {{repr(context.cross_env_exe)}}
+import os as _os
+if _os.path.exists(_cross_env_exe):
+    executable = _cross_env_exe
+del _os, _cross_env_exe
